The fields-schema check ran from _validate_create_values with an op index,
so a custom field written at create and deleted by a later --remove-field op
was re-reported for the life of the item. An op log is append-only: that field
is already repaired, and flagging op 0 in isolation names a defect that no
longer exists in the compiled item.

On the live tracker that was 26 warnings, every one of them stale: 17 severity
plus 9 core attributes (status, priority, tags, description) across four items
whose repairs had landed months earlier. Those warnings were the only thing
blocking `tracker validate --strict`.

This is the second half of a migration that already happened. f148a13a22 moved
the allowed_statuses check to compiled state for exactly this reason --
"historical ops may have used statuses that were valid when written but are now
restricted" -- and compile_ops is already called in validate_ops_file. The
fields check simply never came along.

compile_ops applies per-key last-write-wins, so remove-then-re-add is still
flagged: "some op removed it" is not repair, the last write decides. The
required-field check gets stricter as a side effect -- a required field deleted
by a later op used to pass because op 0 had it, and now fails.

Live warnings 26 -> 0; `tracker validate --strict` passes.

Two real defects surfaced and were repaired rather than silenced:
  * severity, on 17 invariants, written by everything and read by nothing.
    Dropped: priority is the only ranking axis, and a second one invites
    splitting hairs between two P2s.
  * INV-hazov carried pr_ref in BOTH homes, with the core attribute null and
    the field holding 9e3a93a75b. Deleting the field alone would have lost the
    reference, so the value was migrated to the core attribute first.

